---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/lc/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/lc/Footer.astro';
import Container from '../../components/lc/Container.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/lc/ArticleHeader.astro';
import Stats from '../../components/lc/Stats.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/lc/Prose.astro';
import Label from '../../components/lc/Label.astro';
import CtaPanel from '../../components/lc/CtaPanel.astro';
import Button from '../../components/lc/Button.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import { facts } from '../../data/facts';

// Every number, title, name and date on this page comes from src/data/facts.ts.
// Do not retype one here - npm run check:facts will fail the build.
---

<Layout
  title="Discover India Trip Assignment System"
  description={`An automated assignment engine plus a ${facts.discoverIndia.manualPages}-page operations manual that placed ${facts.discoverIndia.students} incoming Grade 9 students across ${facts.discoverIndia.trips} 'Week Without Walls' trips at ${facts.aisc.schoolShort} Chennai, balancing capacity, gender, and ranked preferences.`}
>
  <Nav />
  <main>

    <ArticleHeader
      back={{ href: '/work', label: 'Back to all work' }}
      label="Systems · Google Sheets + Apps Script"
      sublabel="Chapter 1 · Prove it on one hard problem"
      title="Discover India Trip Assignment System"
    >
      The assignment engine behind {facts.aisc.schoolShort}'s "Week Without Walls", placing {facts.discoverIndia.students} incoming Grade 9 students
      across {facts.discoverIndia.trips} experiential trips by ranked preference, capacity, and balance, with a {facts.discoverIndia.manualPages}-page manual
      so the program runs without its author.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <Container>
      <article class="flex max-w-3xl flex-col gap-lc-8 pt-lc-8">
        <Stats
          items={[
          { value: facts.discoverIndia.students, label: 'Students assigned' },
          { value: facts.discoverIndia.trips, label: 'Trips · 3 pillars' },
          { value: facts.discoverIndia.signUps, label: 'Sign-up responses' },
          { value: facts.discoverIndia.manualPages, label: 'Pages of docs' },
          ]}
        />

        <Prose>

          <Label>Overview</Label>
          <h2>What it is</h2>
            <p>
              Discover India is {facts.aisc.schoolShort}'s "Week Without Walls" program, the experiential trips that define a
              student's first year of high school. For SY 2026–27, that meant placing
              <strong>{facts.discoverIndia.students} incoming Grade 9 students</strong> across
              <strong>{facts.discoverIndia.trips} trips</strong> spanning three pillars: Adventure, Service, and
              Culture. I built the complete assignment algorithm and a {facts.discoverIndia.manualPages}-page operations manual that turns
              a sprawling, high-stakes placement problem into a repeatable, documented process.
            </p>
            <p>
              I built the backend assignment engine; Riedwaan Kader was the student-facing lead. And it
              wasn't purely a desk project, I also went on the trip itself as a chaperone in September 2025.
            </p>

          <Label>The Problem</Label>
          <h2>A high-stakes placement done by hand</h2>
            <p>
              Assigning an entire grade to experiential trips is one of the hardest logistics problems a
              school faces. Every student ranks their preferences; every trip has a hard capacity (20
              students and 3 teachers) and needs gender balance; and the result has to feel fair to {facts.discoverIndia.students}
              teenagers and their parents. Done by hand, it's days of work, opaque decisions, and no clean
              way to explain why any one student ended up where they did.
            </p>
            <p>
              It also wasn't durable. The knowledge lived in one person's spreadsheet and one person's head,
              which is exactly the kind of single point of failure a school's signature program shouldn't have.
            </p>

        <Screenshot
          src="/images/work/discover-india-dashboard.png"
          alt={`The Discover India capacity dashboard showing fill rates across ${facts.discoverIndia.trips} trips`}
          caption={`Fill rates across all ${facts.discoverIndia.trips} trips, live during the placement run.`}
        />

          <Label>What I Built</Label>
          <h2>An assignment algorithm plus a full operations manual</h2>
            <p>
              The system runs on Google Sheets, Google Forms, and Apps Script, native to the school's
              workspace, and pairs the automation with the documentation a program needs to outlive its builder:
            </p>
          <ul>
            {[
              ['Student sign-up form', `A Google Form that collected ${facts.discoverIndia.signUps} ranked-preference responses from incoming Grade 9 students.`],
              ['Teacher sign-up form', 'A parallel form to staff each trip with the right faculty leads.'],
              ['Assignment algorithm', 'Places every student by ranked preference while enforcing capacity (20 students / 3 teachers per trip) and gender balance.'],
              ['Capacity-tracked spreadsheet', `A live dashboard showing fill rates across all ${facts.discoverIndia.trips} trips so no trip over- or under-fills.`],
              ['Appeals process', 'A formal, documented path for students to request a change after assignments go out.'],
              [`${facts.discoverIndia.manualPages}-page operations manual`, 'Step-by-step documentation so the program runs the same way next year, with or without me.'],
            ].map(([title, desc]) => (
              <li><strong>{title}.</strong> {desc}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p>
            It was deliberately built for handover in April–May 2026, scope comparable to the SEVAI system,
            but at grade-wide scale and with a much heavier documentation layer.
          </p>

        <Screenshot
          src="/images/work/discover-india-manual.png"
          alt={`A page from the ${facts.discoverIndia.manualPages}-page Discover India operations manual`}
          caption="A page from the operations manual. The documentation is the part that made it survive my leaving."
        />

          <Label>Impact &amp; Results</Label>
          <h2>An entire grade placed, fairly and on the record</h2>
          <Stats
            items={[
              { value: facts.discoverIndia.students, label: 'Students placed', note: 'entire incoming Grade 9' },
              { value: facts.discoverIndia.trips, label: 'Trips assigned', note: 'Adventure · Service · Culture' },
              { value: facts.discoverIndia.manualPages, label: 'Pages of docs', note: 'built for handover' },
            ]}
          />
            <p>
              The system placed all {facts.discoverIndia.students} students across {facts.discoverIndia.trips} trips by ranked preference while holding capacity
              and gender-balance rules consistently, and, just as important, it made every placement
              explainable. The capacity dashboard and appeals process turned a process that used to live in
              one head into something the whole program team could see and trust.
            </p>
            <p>
              Because it shipped with {facts.discoverIndia.manualPages} pages of documentation, {facts.aisc.schoolShort}'s signature Grade 9 trip program can
              keep running long after I leave, the clearest test of whether a system has actually become
              institutional infrastructure.
            </p>

          <Label>Lessons Learned</Label>
          <h2>What I'd carry forward</h2>
          <ul>
            {[
              ['Fairness has to be legible.', `For a placement ${facts.discoverIndia.students} families will scrutinize, being fair isn't enough, the system has to make it visible why each student landed where they did.`],
              ['Documentation is the deliverable.', `${facts.discoverIndia.manualPages} pages of operations manual is what separates a clever one-off script from a program the school can run again next year.`],
              ['Build it, then go live it.', 'Chaperoning the trip I\'d assigned students to closed the loop, the logistics only matter because of the experience they make possible.'],
            ].map(([title, desc]) => (
              <li><strong set:html={title} /> <span set:html={desc} /></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p>
            Discover India is the largest of a family of assignment engines I built, see the
            <a href="/work/student-assignment-systems">Student Assignment Systems</a> overview.
          </p>

        </Prose>
      </article>
    </Container>

    <CtaPanel title="Running a trip or program that's placed by hand every year?">
      <Fragment slot="actions">
        <Button href="/work-with-me#contact">Let's Talk</Button>
        <Button href="/work" variant="secondary" tone="dark">More Case Studies</Button>
      </Fragment>
    </CtaPanel>

  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
